['In Labrador retrievers, two genes determine coat color. The B and b genes control melanin levels, with B being dominant for black and b being recessive for brown. The E and e genes, which code for pigment deposition, are epistatic to the melanin genes, resulting in a yellow coat if the E allele is absent.', 'In Labrador retrievers, two genes determine coat color: one for melanin (B/b) and one for pigment deposition (E/e). The dominant allele B codes for black fur, while b codes for brown. If the dominant allele E is not present, regardless of the melanin gene, the fur will be yellow.', 'Two genes (B and E) determine Labrador retriever coat color. Gene B codes for black or brown fur, while gene E determines pigment deposition. If the dominant E allele is absent, the fur will be yellow, regardless of the B allele.', 'Two genes determine Labrador Retriever coat color: one for melanin amount (B and b) and another for pigment deposition (E and e). The dominant allele E is epistatic, meaning it overrides the other genes effects, resulting in yellow fur if not present, while B and b determine black or brown color.', 'In Labrador retrievers, two genes determine coat color: one for melanin (B/b) and one for pigment deposition (E/e). The dominant allele B codes for black fur, while the recessive allele b codes for brown hair. The E allele is epistatic, meaning it can mask the effect of the B/b gene, resulting in yellow fur if its not present.', 'Two genes (B and E) determine coat color in Labradors, with B and b controlling melanin levels (black or brown) and E and e controlling pigment deposition (epistatic). The dominant E allele is necessary for any color other than yellow, regardless of the B and b genotype.', 'Two genes determine Labrador retriever coat color, one for melanin (B/b) and one for pigment deposition (E/e). The dominant allele B codes for black fur, while the recessive allele b codes for brown. The E allele is epistatic, meaning that if its not present, the fur will be yellow, regardless of the genotype at the black/brown locus.', 'In Labrador Retrievers, two genes determine coat color: one for melanin (B/b) and one for pigment deposition (E/e). The B allele codes for black, while b codes for brown, and E is epistatic to these genes, making it dominant over them, resulting in yellow fur if present.', 'Two genes (B and E) determine coat color in Labrador retrievers. The B allele codes for black fur, while the b allele codes for brown hair. The E allele determines pigment deposition, and if absent, the fur will be yellow, regardless of the B or b allele.', 'Two genes determine coat color in Labradors: one for melanin (B/b) and one for pigment deposition (E/e). The dominant allele E is epistatic, meaning it masks the effects of the other genes, resulting in yellow fur if present, while the recessive allele b codes for brown hair.', 'In Labrador retrievers, two genes (B and E) determine coat color. The B allele codes for black fur, while the b allele codes for brown hair, with E being epistatic to B/b, meaning that if E is absent, the fur will be yellow.', 'In Labrador retrievers, two genes (B and E) determine coat color. The B allele codes for black fur, while the b allele codes for brown hair, with the E allele being epistatic to the black/brown gene, making yellow fur appear if not present.', 'Two genes determine Labrador retriever coat color, one for melanin amount and one for pigment deposition. The dominant allele B codes for black fur, while b codes for brown hair, and E codes for pigment deposition. If the dominant allele E is not present, the fur will be yellow, regardless of the black/brown genotype.', 'In Labrador Retrievers, two genes determine coat color: one for melanin (B and b) and one for pigment deposition (E and e). The dominant allele B codes for black fur, while the recessive allele b codes for brown. The E allele is epistatic, meaning that if its not present, the fur will be yellow, regardless of the melanin genotype.']
