ructures in response to similar environmental pressures. The wings of an insect and a bird are a classic example of convergent evolution, as they share similar functions (flight) despite being from different evolutionary lineages.', 'Convergent evolution is the process where different species, not closely related, independently develop similar traits or structures in response to similar environmental pressures. The example of convergent evolution is the wings of an insect and a bird, which have evolved separately but share similar functions and structures despite being from different species.', 'Convergent evolution is the process by which different species, not closely related, independently develop similar traits or features in response to similar environmental pressures. A classic example of convergent evolution is the development of wings in insects and birds, despite being from different evolutionary lineages.', 'Convergent evolution is the process where different species, not closely related, develop similar traits or structures in response to similar environmental pressures. The wings of an insect and a bird are a classic example of convergent evolution, as they have evolved independently to serve the same purpose (flight) despite being from different animal groups.', 'Convergent evolution is the process where different species, not closely related, develop similar traits in response to similar environmental pressures. The example of convergent evolution is the wings of an insect and the wings of a bird, which have similar structures and functions despite being unrelated species.', 'Convergent evolution refers to the process where different species, not closely related, develop similar traits or features in response to similar environmental pressures. A classic example of convergent evolution is the wings of an insect and the wings of a bird, which have similar functions and structures despite being unrelated species.']
