istics in response to similar environmental pressures. The wings of an insect and a bird are a classic example of convergent evolution, as they have similar structures and functions despite being distinct species.', 'Convergent evolution refers to the independent evolution of similar traits in different species, often in response to similar environmental pressures. The wings of an insect and a bird are a classic example of convergent evolution, as they both have evolved to serve the same function (flight) despite being very distant species.', 'Convergent evolution is the process by which different species, lineages, or organisms independently evolve similar traits or characteristics in response to similar environmental pressures or selection forces. The wings of an insect and the wings of a bird are a classic example of convergent evolution, as they have developed similar structures and functions despite being derived from different ancestral forms.', 'Convergent evolution is the process by which different species, lineages, or organisms independently evolve similar traits or characteristics in response to similar environmental pressures or selection forces. The wings of an insect and the wings of a bird are a classic example of convergent evolution, as they have evolved to perform a similar function (flight) despite being composed of different materials and structures.']
